  "summary": "Portrait lighting with a triangular patch of light on the shadowed cheek opposite the light source",
  "description": "Creates depth and psychological intensity by illuminating one side of the face while leaving the other in shadow, developed by Rembrandt to enhance emotional expression and three-dimensionality in his portraits.",
  "culturalContext": "Rembrandt lighting — the triangular cheek-patch produced by a single high-side key light — is named for the painter whose Amsterdam studio portraits (self-portraits above all) canonized the look: the eye on the shadow side still lit, the rest falling into warm dark. Hollywood portraiture (the \"Rembrandt key\" in studio grammar) adopted it as flattering-realism default; the triangle remains the first portrait-lighting setup every photographer learns.",
